# Interview Guide — MiniGo AI

## 30-Second Explanation

I built a competitive AI for a 5×5 version of Go using Minimax search with Alpha-Beta pruning. I implemented the entire game engine, including captures, liberties, Ko, suicide prevention, and legal move generation. To make the AI competitive under strict runtime constraints, I designed and tuned several heuristic evaluation functions based on Go strategy, including eye detection, shape analysis, positional weighting, and liberty evaluation. After completing the AI, I expanded the project into a browser-based application so users could play against the engine through an interactive interface.

---

## 2-Minute Explanation

The goal of this project was to explore adversarial search and optimization techniques by building a competitive Go-playing AI from scratch.

The first challenge was implementing the Go rule engine itself. I created board representations, legal move generation, liberty detection, capture mechanics, Ko rule enforcement, suicide prevention, and scoring.

Once the engine was complete, I implemented a Minimax search algorithm with Alpha-Beta pruning. Since Go has an extremely large branching factor, searching the full game tree is impossible, so I developed a heuristic evaluation function that estimates board strength. The evaluation combines positional weighting, liberty analysis, eye detection, shape analysis, center control, and game-phase-specific heuristics. I experimentally tuned these weights by testing against competing agents and removing heuristics that did not improve performance.

To satisfy the assignment's runtime constraints, I implemented move ordering, adaptive search depth near the endgame, and profiled the code to identify performance bottlenecks. The final system consistently generated moves in under one second while outperforming most competing class agents.

After finishing the original assignment, I expanded the project into a browser-based interface using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to make the AI easier to interact with and to prepare for backend integration.

