## Common Interview Questions

### Why Minimax?

Go is a deterministic, turn-based, zero-sum game, making Minimax a natural choice because it models optimal play by assuming both players always make their best possible moves.

### Why Alpha-Beta Pruning?

Plain Minimax becomes computationally expensive because the search space grows exponentially. Alpha-Beta pruning eliminates branches that cannot influence the final decision, allowing deeper searches within the same runtime budget.

### Why only depth 3?

The course imposed a strict per-move time limit. Depth 3 provided the best balance between search quality and execution speed. Near the end of the game, I dynamically increased the search depth because there were fewer legal moves to evaluate.

### What was the hardest part?

Designing the heuristic evaluation function. Programming Minimax is relatively straightforward compared to determining how to evaluate Go positions effectively. The most difficult heuristics involved liberties and eye detection because they required domain-specific knowledge of Go strategy.

### What optimization are you most proud of?

Move ordering. By evaluating strong candidate moves first, Alpha-Beta pruning became significantly more effective, allowing the search to explore deeper positions without exceeding the runtime limit.

### If you continued the project, what would you add?

I would implement transposition tables with Zobrist hashing, iterative deepening, Monte Carlo Tree Search for comparison, complete REST backend integration, and support for larger board sizes.

### What did this project teach you?

This project taught me how to combine algorithms, optimization, and software engineering to solve a computationally difficult problem. It also reinforced the importance of heuristic design, profiling, and iterative performance tuning when exact solutions are computationally infeasible.
